.TH TR 1
.SH NAME
tr \- translate characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tr
.I string1 string2
.br
.B tr \-d
.I string1
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tr
copies the standard input to the standard output with
substitution or deletion of selected bytes.
Input bytes found in
.I string1
are mapped into the corresponding bytes of
.IR string2 .
When
.I string2
is short it is padded to the length of
.I string1
by duplicating its last byte.
The option
.B \-d
deletes from the input all bytes in
.IR string1 .
It is recognised only as the first argument.
.PP
In either string a sequence
.IB x - y\fR,
where
.I x
and
.I y
are single characters,
stands for the range of bytes from
.I x
through
.IR y .
.B \en
stands for a newline and
.B \et
for a tab.
A
.B \e
followed by any other character stands for that character.
.SH EXAMPLES
Replace all upper-case
letters by lower-case.
.IP
.EX
tr A-Z a-z <mixed >lower
.EE
.PP
Drop the carriage returns from a file that has them.
.IP
.EX
tr -d '\er' <crlf >lf
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/tr/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sed (1)
.SH BUGS
.I Tr
works on bytes, not runes.
There is no
.B \-c
and no
.BR \-s .
